/**
  @page guides-io Input, output, and visualization
  @brief Choose a file format, export a field, and inspect the result.

  Mesh files describe geometry and topology. Field files describe values in a
  finite element space. Visualization output brings the two together so a
  viewer can interpret a field on its mesh.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-io-formats Choose a format

  Select the format according to the required mesh, field or visualization
  operation.
  MFEM and MEDIT both use `.mesh`, so mesh loading requires an explicit format.
  XDMF output uses the dedicated `IO::XDMF` writer.

  | Task | Starting point | Files |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | View a computed field in ParaView | `IO::XDMF` | XML metadata and HDF5 data |
  | Exchange a mesh with MMG | `IO::FileFormat::MEDIT` | MEDIT mesh |
  | Exchange MFEM-compatible meshes and fields | `IO::FileFormat::MFEM` | Mesh and grid-function files |
  | Store supported mesh or field data in binary form | `IO::FileFormat::HDF5` | HDF5 data |

  Format support depends on the data being stored. See the
  @ref guides-io-meshformats "mesh-format guide" and
  @ref guides-io-gridfunctions "grid-function guide" for the relevant interfaces.
  A complete simulation checkpoint includes the saved fields, application state and
  restart logic.

  @section guides-io-figures Plot colors and presentation

  Solution fields and numerical plots use the **Turbo** colormap. In ParaView,
  choose **Turbo** from the color-map presets. Comparison panels retain the same
  field range so that equal values have equal colors. Signed fields use a
  symmetric range about zero.

  Line plots sample their curve colors from Turbo and distinguish series with
  markers and line styles. Mesh edges, reference outlines and categorical
  material regions use separate, consistent colors. Figure backgrounds remain
  transparent; captions and labels use the body font, Manrope, with Computer
  Modern LaTeX lettering for mathematical expressions. Text adapts to the light
  or dark theme while field colors remain unchanged. Documentation videos use
  30 or 60 fps and preserve simulation-time playback.

  @section guides-io-xdmf-overview Export a field for visualization

  XDMF separates the description of a dataset from its numerical data. Open
  the `.xdmf` metadata file in ParaView; keep the referenced HDF5 files beside it.
  Link the application against `Rodin::IO` to use the writer.

  The following complete program interpolates the known field @f$u(x,y)=xy@f$
  on a triangular mesh of the unit square and exports it. It needs no PDE solve,
  making it a small check of the visualization workflow.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;

  int main()
  {
    Mesh mesh = Mesh().UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
    mesh.scale(1.0 / 15.0);
    P1 vh(mesh);
    GridFunction field(vh);
    field = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() * p.y(); };

    IO::XDMF xdmf("Field");
    xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", field);
    xdmf.write().flush();
    return 0;
  }
  @endcode

  The writer observes the mesh and field. Keep them alive until output is
  finished; construct them before the writer as shown above. The explicit
  name `"u"` identifies the field in the viewer.

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-overview-paraview Inspect the exported data

  1. Open `Field.xdmf` in ParaView and select **Apply**.
  2. Select the point field **u** for coloring.
  3. Choose the **Turbo** color-map preset.
  4. Use **Surface With Edges** to inspect the mesh.
  5. Optionally apply **Warp By Scalar** to lift the field above the plane.

  For this example, the nodal values range from zero to one. The field is zero
  on the two coordinate axes and reaches one at the opposite corner. This
  provides a simple check that the coordinates and field were exported together.

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-overview-time Record a time series

  Register the mesh and field once, update the field, and call `write(time)`
  after each update. For example, the following fragment writes two snapshots
  of @f$u(x,y,t)=txy@f$ using the objects above.

  @code{.cpp}
  for (const Real time : {0.0, 1.0})
  {
    field = [time](const Geometry::Point& p)
    {
      return time * p.x() * p.y();
    };
    xdmf.write(time);
  }
  xdmf.flush();
  @endcode

  This fragment replaces the single `write()` call in the complete program.
  Each call records the current data. For changing geometry, named grids,
  multiple fields, and mesh policies, see @subpage guides-io-xdmf.

  @section guides-io-mesh-io Exchange a mesh

  Loading a MEDIT mesh and saving it in MFEM format uses two explicit format
  selections.

  @code{.cpp}
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh.load("input.mesh", IO::FileFormat::MEDIT);
  mesh.save("output.mesh", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  @endcode

  Conversion transfers the existing mesh through the selected file interfaces.
  Format-specific metadata are mapped to the target representation. See @subpage guides-io-meshformats.

  @section guides-io-gf Store a finite element field

  A saved coefficient field must be interpreted with its mesh, finite element
  space, and numbering. Preserve those together when exchanging field data.
  A grid function can be saved and loaded using the supported format interfaces.

  @code{.cpp}
  field.save("field.gf", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  field.load("field.gf", IO::FileFormat::MFEM);
  @endcode

  The receiving field must have a compatible space. See
  @subpage guides-io-gridfunctions for complete save/load examples.

  @section guides-io-see-also Continue with a complete solve

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" exports a computed PDE
  solution using this workflow. The @ref examples-io-index "I/O examples"
  cover mesh exchange and time-dependent visualization separately.
*/
